{"name":"Wheel of Dharma","hex":"#ffcd00","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wheel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A circular device capable of rotating on its axis, facilitating movement or transportation or performing labour in machines.\n\n A steering wheel and its implied control of a vehicle.","A steering wheel and its implied control of a vehicle."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To roll along on wheels.","To transport something or someone using any wheeled mechanism, such as a wheelchair."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wheel"},{"term":"dharma","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The natural order of the universe; natural law, cosmic order.","One's obligation in respect to one's position in society, or the universe; one's duty, societally or cosmically."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dharma"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dharmachakra","blocks":[{"text":"The dharmachakra or wheel of dharma is a symbol used in the Dharmic religions. It has a widespread use in Buddhism. The dharmachakra is considered one of the ashtamangala in Hinduism and Buddhism. In some specific regional sites like in Odisha, dharmachakra imagery in Hindu temples has been linked to sites with an earlier Buddhist presence. The symbol also finds its usage in modern India, most notably at the centre of the flag of India."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Ashoka_Chakra_Y.svg/330px-Ashoka_Chakra_Y.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dharmachakra"},{"title":"Wheel","blocks":[{"text":"A wheel is a rotating component intended to turn on an axle bearing. The wheel is one of the key components of the wheel and axle, which is one of the six simple machines. Wheels, in conjunction with axles, allow heavy objects to be moved easily, facilitating transportation, supporting loads, and performing work in machines. Wheels are also used for other purposes, such as a ship's wheel, steering wheel, potter's wheel, and flywheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_Kelten_011.4.jpg/330px-Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_Kelten_011.4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheel"},{"title":"Dharma","blocks":[{"text":"Dharma is a key concept in various Indian-origin religions. The term dharma does not have a single, clear translation and conveys a multifaceted idea. Etymologically, it comes from the Sanskrit dhr-, meaning to hold or to support, thus referring to the law that sustains things—from one's life to society, and to the universe at large. Most commonly, dharma refers to an individual's moral responsibilities or duties; the dharma of a farmer differs from the dharma of a soldier, thus making the concept a dynamic one. The antonym of dharma is adharma."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dharma"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/The_%27Ten_Indus_Scripts%27_discovered_near_the_northern_gateway_of_the_Dholavira_citadel.jpg/500px-The_%27Ten_Indus_Scripts%27_discovered_near_the_northern_gateway_of_the_Dholavira_citadel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/The_%27Ten_Indus_Scripts%27_discovered_near_the_northern_gateway_of_the_Dholavira_citadel.jpg/500px-The_%27Ten_Indus_Scripts%27_discovered_near_the_northern_gateway_of_the_Dholavira_citadel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ten Indus characters from the northern gate of Dholavira, dubbed the Dholavira Signboard.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_'Ten_Indus_Scripts'_discovered_near_the_northern_gateway_of_the_Dholavira_citadel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Dharmachakra_on_Pillar_-_South_Face_-_West_Pillar_-_South_Gateway_-_Stupa_1_-_Sanchi_Hill_2013-02-21_4355.JPG/500px-Dharmachakra_on_Pillar_-_South_Face_-_West_Pillar_-_South_Gateway_-_Stupa_1_-_Sanchi_Hill_2013-02-21_4355.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Dharmachakra_on_Pillar_-_South_Face_-_West_Pillar_-_South_Gateway_-_Stupa_1_-_Sanchi_Hill_2013-02-21_4355.JPG/1280px-Dharmachakra_on_Pillar_-_South_Face_-_West_Pillar_-_South_Gateway_-_Stupa_1_-_Sanchi_Hill_2013-02-21_4355.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Dharmachakra_on_Pillar_-_South_Face_-_West_Pillar_-_South_Gateway_-_Stupa_1_-_Sanchi_Hill_2013-02-21_4355.JPG/500px-Dharmachakra_on_Pillar_-_South_Face_-_West_Pillar_-_South_Gateway_-_Stupa_1_-_Sanchi_Hill_2013-02-21_4355.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Worshipers and Dharmachakra, Sanchi Stupa, South Face, West Pillar.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADharmachakra_on_Pillar_-_South_Face_-_West_Pillar_-_South_Gateway_-_Stupa_1_-_Sanchi_Hill_2013-02-21_4355.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Sarnath_Ashoka_Lions_with_Dharma_Wheel.jpg/500px-Sarnath_Ashoka_Lions_with_Dharma_Wheel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Sarnath_Ashoka_Lions_with_Dharma_Wheel.jpg/500px-Sarnath_Ashoka_Lions_with_Dharma_Wheel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The original Lion Capital of Ashoka, from Sarnath. It originally supported a large dharmachakra on the top (reconstitution).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASarnath_Ashoka_Lions_with_Dharma_Wheel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common